# =============================================================================
# analysis.R
# Workshop 5: EEG frontal alpha asymmetry (FA) with the Neuroelectrics Enobio 8
# (NIC2 and Lab Streaming Layer) in iMotions Lab 11.1, two video commercials,
# analysis session
# Martin's Family Clothing Consumer Behavior Research Laboratory, JSU
#
# SIMULATED DATA. Every number this script reads was generated by
# make_simulated_data.py (seed 20267088). No person was recorded. The design copies the logic
# of the iMotions demo study "DEMO - SonyBravia" (a commercial with a key scene against the same
# commercial without it, between subjects; two commercials within subjects; two neutral 1 minute
# nature videos as baselines; the last 10 seconds of each commercial analyzed). Nothing about the
# content of the Sony commercials was used.
#
# Design. 48 respondents (P01 to P48) were randomized 24 and 24 to Version A (Commercial_1A, WITH
# the key scene at 32 to 38 s) or Version B (Commercial_1B, the same commercial with the 6 s cut).
# Everyone also saw Commercial_2. Within each version, half saw Commercial 1 first. Flow:
# Baseline_1 (60 s nature video), first commercial, rating slide, Baseline_2 (60 s nature video),
# second commercial, rating slide. Each commercial is compared with the baseline that immediately
# precedes it. The Group column holds version and order together (for example A_C1first).
#
# Frontal alpha asymmetry (FA). The iMotions EEG Frontal Asymmetry R Notebook computes, in each
# 1 s Welch window, FA = ln(alpha power at the RIGHT frontal electrode) - ln(alpha power at the LEFT
# frontal electrode). Alpha power is inversely related to brain activity, so a POSITIVE change
# means relatively MORE LEFT frontal activity, read as approach motivation; a negative change means
# relatively more right frontal activity, read as withdrawal. Approach is not the same as liking.
# FA scores are interpreted relative to each other (here: relative to a baseline), never as
# absolute values.
#
# Channel map (ASSUMED, verify in NIC2 before reusing this script): Ch1 Fz, Ch2 Cz, Ch3 Pz, Ch4 Oz,
# Ch5 F3, Ch6 F4, Ch7 P3, Ch8 P4 (the lab manual montage order). The FA notebook was run with
# left = Ch5 (F3) and right = Ch6 (F4), Alpha band, Welch 1 s windows, notch 60 Hz, band-pass
# 0.5 - 100 Hz, artifact threshold 120 microvolts. Its Enobio defaults (left Ch1, right Ch7) would
# be Fz and P3 in this montage; step 12 shows what that mistake does.
#
# Hypotheses (all tests two tailed, alpha = .05; the H1 and H2 one tailed p values are printed for
# teaching only):
#   H1 (within, Version A): FA during the KeyScene minus FA during the baseline that preceded
#      Commercial_1A is greater than zero. One sample t on the change (the same as a paired t),
#      Cohen's d_z with a 95% CI, Wilcoxon signed rank check.
#   H2 (between): the baseline corrected FA during the EndScene (last 10 s) of Commercial 1 is
#      higher for Version A than for Version B. Welch t, Cohen's d and Hedges' g with 95% CIs,
#      Mann Whitney check, and a power note (n per group for 80% power at the observed d).
#   H3 (within, all kept respondents): the baseline corrected whole commercial FA differs between
#      Commercial_2 and Commercial_1. Paired t and d_z, plus the linear mixed model
#      FA_change ~ commercial * version + position + (1 | respondent) and the ICC.
#   Exploratory: whole commercial FA change with liking (each commercial); frontal midline theta
#      (Ch1, Fz) change from baseline during the commercials, descriptive only.
#   Sensitivity: H1 and H2 with a stricter exclusion threshold (15 percent) and without baseline
#      correction.
#   Teaching contrast: H1 again on the export made with the notebook DEFAULT channels.
#
# Exclusion rule, written before data collection: exclude a respondent when more than 25 percent
# of the F3 or F4 samples were removed (artifact threshold) in any baseline or commercial, or when
# the session log records a Red NIC2 quality status at F3 or F4 that could not be fixed.
#
# What the script does, in order:
#   STEP 0.  Load packages, set paths, the figure theme, and helper functions
#   STEP 1.  Read the six files (FA export, FA export with default channels, PSD export, artifact
#            report, session log, survey)
#   STEP 2.  Check the channel map and the notebook settings
#   STEP 3.  Apply the exclusion rule (artifact report and session log)
#   STEP 4.  Check the files against each other
#   STEP 5.  Build one row per kept respondent and compute the baseline corrected scores
#   STEP 6.  Descriptive statistics (SIMULATED_descriptives.csv)
#   STEP 7.  H1: key scene versus its baseline (Version A)
#   STEP 8.  H2: end scene of Commercial 1, Version A versus Version B, with a power note
#   STEP 9.  H3: Commercial_2 versus Commercial_1 (paired), mixed model, ICC
#   STEP 10. Exploratory: liking, frontal midline theta
#   STEP 11. Sensitivity: 15 percent threshold, no baseline correction
#   STEP 12. Teaching contrast: the notebook default channels (Ch1 and Ch7)
#   STEP 13. Assumption checks (figure)
#   STEP 14. Report figures (PNG, 1400 px wide, 150 dpi)
#   STEP 15. Summary tables (SIMULATED_test_results.csv, SIMULATED_model_results.csv)
#
# MODELED layouts (verify on the lab computer): the artifact report table, the session log, the
# survey, the Type values ("Stimulus", "Annotation"), Parent Stimulus on stimulus rows, the PSD
# column order, and the single "Algorithm Device Name" column. The FA and PSD column names are the
# ones in the R Processed EEG Export Files article.
#
# How to run (either way works):
#   a) In RStudio: open this file, set the working directory to the folder that holds the CSV
#      files (Session > Set Working Directory > To Source File Location), then Source.
#   b) From a terminal, inside the data folder:  Rscript analysis.R
# The console output is also written to analysis_output.txt next to the data.
#
# Packages used: readr, dplyr, tidyr, ggplot2, lme4, lmerTest. Effect sizes and their confidence
# intervals are computed by hand below (no effect size package is needed; if the effectsize
# package happens to be installed, step 7 uses it only as a cross-check). If a package is missing,
# run once in the console:
#   install.packages(c("readr", "dplyr", "tidyr", "ggplot2", "lme4", "lmerTest"))
# =============================================================================

